BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sphera-media-lab-2026-open-call-discourse@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sphera Media Lab 2026: Open Call for Masterclasses and Ke
 ynotes
DESCRIPTION:European Lab and the Sphera Network are opening submissions for
  the discourse programme of the next Sphera Media Lab\, the annual forum f
 or people defending independent media in Europe. THEMES: technological ind
 ependence (sovereignty\, tools\, mutualisation)\; economic independence (c
 ooperative models)\; editorial independence (press freedom\, resisting the
  far right). FORMATS SOUGHT: masterclasses\, led by an expert sharing adva
 nced knowledge\, technique and hands-on insight\; and keynotes\, inspiring
  talks grounded in a specific case study. WHO CAN PROPOSE: media outlets\,
  associations\, researchers and professionals. The organisers state they p
 ay particular attention to parity and diversity across their programmes an
 d ask that proposals take those criteria into account. WHAT IS COVERED: se
 lected contributors travel to Brussels with transport\, accommodation and 
 a fee covered. THE EVENT: Sphera Media Lab 2026 runs on 3 December 2026 at
  LaVallee in Brussels\, a full day of conferences\, masterclasses and work
 shops for independent media professionals from across Europe\, alongside t
 he premiere of a documentary on threats facing European journalists. APPLI
 CATION: submit your masterclass or keynote proposal via the Typeform at ar
 ty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM before 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: 
 European Lab (Arty Farty) and the Sphera Network\, coordinated by Medianes
 \nLocation: LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. 
 Proposals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should
  reflect a European perspective.\nAward: Selected contributors receive a f
 ee plus travel to Brussels and accommodation\, all covered by the organise
 rs. The fee amount is not published in the call. No submission fee.\nDurat
 ion: One-day event on 3 December 2026\; contributors deliver a single mast
 erclass or keynote\n\nMore: https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. Prop
 osals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should ref
 lect a European perspective.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iclc-2027-antwerp-call-for-participation@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: ICLC 2027: International Conference on Live Coding\, Call
  for Participation (Dialogues)
DESCRIPTION:The International Conference on Live Coding is dedicated to pra
 ctices and research focused on technologies and philosophies that interpre
 t the use of computer code and the public writing of algorithms as perform
 ative gestures. The 10th edition runs 9 to 13 March 2027 in Antwerp\, host
 ed by Lambdasonic (TOPLAP Belgium) with the Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp
  and De Singel. THEME: 'Dialogues'\, framing live coding as continuous imp
 rovisational exchange between coder\, machine and audience\, and across di
 sciplines. Suggested topics include dialogues between live coding and acou
 stic musicians\, dancers\, theatre\, classically trained musicians reading
  live-coded (or graphical) scores\, band and jazz-combo settings\, live-co
 der ensembles\, ten-minute live-coded mini-operas\, DMX lighting systems\,
  graphics and shader live coding\, robotics (pen plotters\, textile-weavin
 g robots)\, design-as-performance\, community building\, diversity/equity/
 inclusion\, accessibility of live coding languages and interfaces\, educat
 ion\, and algorave. The venues will facilitate collaborations with residen
 t dancers\, a jazz combo (back-line provided: drums\, guitar amp\, bass am
 p\, piano)\, a string quartet reading scores a prima vista\, visual artist
 s\, students and researchers where possible. FORMATS: short papers (2-4 pa
 ges) and long papers (5-12 pages) using the provided template\; live perfo
 rmances (800-word abstract\, max 150-word programme note\, technical rider
 )\; workshops (short 2h or long 4h\, max 700-word abstract\, held in Engli
 sh or Dutch)\; installations (max 700-word abstract\, technical questionna
 ire plus a measured plan or sketch\, with the interactive aspect clearly d
 efined)\; and satellite events. All contributors submit a max 150-word bio
 graphy. Peer review is single-blind with at least two reviewers per submis
 sion. LANGUAGE: submissions must be in English\, except that live performa
 nce\, installation and workshop proposals may also be submitted in another
  language (workshops: English\, Dutch or French) with a machine-translated
  English version\, stated as such. Substantial use of generative AI in wri
 ting papers and performance abstracts must be disclosed\; proofreading and
  translation are exempt. In-person presentation is encouraged\, with remot
 e proposals considered and sessions live-streamed where possible\; the org
 anisers ask attendees to minimise travel impact and to link up with nearby
  satellite events to turn a long trip into a mini-tour. The call notes att
 ention to access needs\, care responsibilities and other barriers to parti
 cipation. SATELLITE EVENTS: live coding nodes\, art schools and other orga
 nisations worldwide are invited to host their own events before or after I
 CLC 2027\; submit a declaration of interest via Fourwaves before 1 Februar
 y 2027 with a principal contact\, city and any confirmed details\, and joi
 n #iclc-satellite-events on the TOPLAP Discord. Hosts are asked to seek lo
 cal funding for accommodation\, travel and artist fees\; the committee can
 not fund or organise these but will coordinate dates and publish your even
 t. TO APPLY: submit via Fourwaves at https://event.fourwaves.com/iclc2027/
 pages using the templates at https://codeberg.org/iclc/iclc-templates (rea
 d the README for full proposal instructions). Submission deadline extended
  to 1 September 2026\; acceptance notification 1 November 2026. CALL FOR R
 EVIEWERS: anyone active in a live-coding-related field can volunteer by em
 ailing a short description and keywords of their expertise to iclc@lambdas
 onic.be\, the same address for general questions.\n\nOrganisation: Lambdas
 onic (TOPLAP Belgium)\, Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel\nLoca
 tion: Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel International Arts Cent
 er\, Antwerp\, Belgium. Open to anyone engaged with live coding\; remote p
 articipation considered.\nAward: NO submission fee. The call does not stat
 e artist fees\, travel support or accommodation for accepted contributors\
 , so budget for self-funded attendance unless the organisers announce othe
 rwise. Proceedings are published with a DOI per article. Satellite-event h
 osts can request a Letter of Intent to support their own local funding app
 lications.\nDuration: Five-day conference\, 9 to 13 March 2027. Workshops 
 are 2 hours (short) or 4 hours (long).\n\nMore: https://iclc.toplap.org/20
 27/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iclc.toplap.org/2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
LOCATION:Royal Conservatoire of Antwerp and De Singel International Arts Ce
 nter\, Antwerp\, Belgium. Open to anyone engaged with live coding\; remote
  participation considered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-heritage-innovation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Heritage Innovation (September 2026 round
 )
DESCRIPTION:Heritage Innovation funds projects and research tracks that con
 tribute to substantive innovation in the heritage sector. Examples given b
 y the fund include developing new narratives about cultural heritage\, str
 engthening regional collaboration across the sector\, redesigning how a co
 llection is presented\, and feasibility studies on technological applicati
 ons such as AI or gamification\, which makes this one of the more tech-adj
 acent Mondriaan schemes. WHO CAN APPLY: museums and collection-holding cul
 tural heritage institutions\, including archives\, in the Netherlands and 
 the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. Cultural organisations in the heritage 
 sector may also apply if they collaborate with one or more collection-hold
 ing institutions. All applicants must be non-profit\, not-for-profit entit
 ies. NOT FUNDED: fixed costs\, restoration\, building museums\, or regular
  operations. CONDITIONS: institutions must subscribe to the Fair Practice 
 Code\, the Culture Governance Code and the Diversity and Inclusion Code\, 
 and must pay visual artists fairly where artists are involved. AMOUNTS: pr
 ojects get up to 40% of variable project-related costs on a minimum projec
 t budget of EUR 25\,000\; research tracks get up to 70% of costs capped at
  EUR 15\,000 with no minimum budget. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund onli
 ne system after registering an account. Deadline 17 September 2026 before 
 16:00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time.\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\n
 Location: Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Neth
 erlands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.\nAward: Projects: up to 40%
  of variable project-related costs\, with a minimum project budget of EUR 
 25\,000. Research tracks: up to 70% of costs\, capped at EUR 15\,000\, wit
 h no minimum budget. No application fee.\nDuration: Project or research tr
 ack. This card covers the 17 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www
 .mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-innovation/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-inno
 vation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Nether
 lands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:video-pool-matr-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Video Pool: Media Art + Technology Residency (MATR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:Video Pool's Media Art + Technology Residency supports experime
 ntal\, independent\, technology-based work. The selected artist or artists
  develop a new project (or adapt and transform an existing one) in direct 
 response to the intimate scale and specific character of the Poolside Gall
 ery\, culminating in an exhibition there. The call is deliberately open on
  medium: video\, sound\, installation\, digital media\, performance\, inte
 ractive technologies\, expanded cinema and interdisciplinary practices are
  all welcome. VP asks proposals to speak clearly to your practice\, the qu
 estions\, materials or ideas that drive the work\, and how that work might
  grow\, shift or take new form inside the gallery. It is explicitly intere
 sted in expansive proposals\, work that pushes past the gallery's four wal
 ls to engage its surrounding spaces (hallways\, windows\, projection wall)
  or that reimagines what the gallery itself can hold\, and in projects tha
 t engage technology as a creative\, critical\, aesthetic or socio-politica
 l tool. Priority is given to artists from equity-seeking communities whose
  practices contribute to the advancement of experimental media arts. Resid
 ents get full access to VP's available equipment\, facilities and technica
 l resources throughout the residency\, and applicants are encouraged to re
 view the equipment and facilities list before proposing. ELIGIBILITY: open
  to VP members (this is a member submission call)\; no nationality restric
 tion is stated\, but the residency and exhibition are in person in Winnipe
 g. APPLICATION: artist bio (1\,500 characters)\, headshot\, description of
  your artistic practice (3\,000 characters)\, project proposal for MATR (3
 \,000 characters)\, equipment and facility needs (750 characters)\, descri
 ption of how you would use the space (750 characters)\, CV\, up to five im
 ages (optional) and up to two video or documentation links. Submit via the
  online form at videopool.org/matr-2027-application/ by 30 August 2026\; a
 lternative submission formats can be arranged by contacting Emma (emma@vid
 eopool.org) or Iyunade (iyunade@videopool.org). General enquiries: info@vi
 deopool.org\, 204-949-9134.\n\nOrganisation: Video Pool Media Arts Centre\
 nLocation: In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gall
 ery\, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.\nAward
 : CAD 2\,250 artist fee\, covering both the residency period and the subse
 quent Poolside Gallery exhibition. Residents also get full access to VP's 
 equipment\, facilities and technical resources\; the Poolside Gallery itse
 lf is equipped with two projectors and four speakers. Applicants must be V
 P members\, so a membership is purchased separately\, but there is no sepa
 rate application fee.\nDuration: Up to two months to develop and install t
 he project\, including one month of dedicated access to the Poolside Galle
 ry. Residency takes place in Winter 2027\; exhibition dates TBD.\n\nMore: 
 https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-applic
 ations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-app
 lications/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gallery
 \, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:knowcase-sonar-cotec-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: KNOWCASE Open Call 2027: permanent digital-culture instal
 lation in Madrid (Sonar+D x Fundacion Cotec)
DESCRIPTION:Fundacion Cotec (a Spanish innovation-focused non-profit) and S
 onar+D\, building on an initial experiment at Sonar 2026\, invite proposal
 s for a long-term installation at KNOWCASE\, the street-level showcase of 
 Cotec's Madrid headquarters in Chamberi\, visible to the public 24 hours a
  day. THEME: digital culture\, meaning projects that combine knowledge\, r
 esearch\, science and data with art\, technology and social impact. Cotec'
 s stated priorities are the big transitions (analogue to digital\, linear 
 to circular economy\, tangible to intangible) plus innovation ecosystems a
 nd R&D&I\, the climate crisis\, the future of work and learning\, social d
 ivides\, citizen participation and democratic health. WHAT YOU GET: a budg
 et of up to EUR 100\,000 plus VAT covering everything (design\, production
 \, installation\, maintenance\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, acc
 ommodation and insurance)\, and a minimum 24-month public display from Feb
 ruary 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, collectives\, designers\, technologis
 ts\, universities\, research centres\, startups\, businesses\, creative ag
 encies\, producers\, cultural institutions and multidisciplinary teams\, i
 nternationally\; applicants must demonstrate solid experience and a releva
 nt portfolio. CONSTRAINTS: the proposal must be original and never previou
 sly exhibited\, must include participative or interactive elements\, must 
 encourage critical thinking\, and must be technically and financially viab
 le for the full 24+ month exhibition period (durability and maintenance ar
 e central selection criteria). Selection weighs conceptual quality highest
  (40 percent)\, followed by public interactivity (25 percent). HOW TO APPL
 Y: one PDF of maximum 15 pages containing summary\, concept\, technical pr
 oposal\, visuals\, budget\, timeline\, team profile\, portfolio and declar
 ation\, in Spanish\, another official language of Spain\, or English\, via
  the application portal linked from the terms and conditions. Results anno
 unced 14 October 2026. Deadline 7 September 2026 at midnight CEST.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Fundacion Cotec and Sonar+D\nLocation: Installation at the KNOWC
 ASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headquarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Sp
 ain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. The call is international\; appli
 cations accepted in Spanish\, other official languages of Spain\, or Engli
 sh.\nAward: Up to EUR 100\,000 (plus VAT)\, all-inclusive: conceptualisati
 on\, design\, technical development\, production\, installation\, maintena
 nce\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, accommodation and insurance.\
 nDuration: Installation displayed for a minimum of 24 months\, starting Fe
 bruary 2027\n\nMore: https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Installation at the KNOWCASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headq
 uarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Spain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. 
 The call is international\; applications accepted in Spanish\, other offic
 ial languages of Spain\, or English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech4dev-ai-cohort-program-3-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project Tech4Dev: AI Cohort Program 3.0 (NGOs building AI
  solutions)
DESCRIPTION:The third round of Project Tech4Dev's AI Cohort\, a hands-on im
 plementation programme for NGOs designing\, building and deploying respons
 ible AI solutions inside their own programmes. Cohort 2.0 worked with seve
 n NGOs across education\, civic engagement\, youth development and nonprof
 it technology\, who built and piloted AI teaching assistants\, volunteer c
 oaches\, mentoring tools and decision-support systems\; the organisers' st
 ated lesson was that adoption turns on understanding user needs\, testing 
 rapidly\, measuring impact and building responsibly rather than on the tec
 hnology itself. Cohort 3.0 is aimed at organisations ready to move past ex
 ploration into solutions that can be deployed in real-world settings. STRU
 CTURE (three sprints over roughly six months): Discovery Sprint (months 1 
 to 2) covering problem discovery\, use-case prioritisation\, data-readines
 s assessment\, user research and solution design\; Build Sprint (months 2 
 to 5) covering prototype development\, implementation sprints\, user testi
 ng\, mentorship and responsible AI evaluations\; and Activation Sprint (mo
 nth 6) covering pilot deployment\, impact assessment\, operationalisation 
 planning and a final showcase with peers and ecosystem partners. SELECTION
  CRITERIA: NGOs must show some in-house technical or data capability\, ide
 ally a dedicated person who can anchor the work (not necessarily an AI exp
 ert\, but able to manage workflows\, communicate operational requirements 
 and work with mentors and experts)\; must commit one person's full bandwid
 th for the whole programme\, including aligning leadership\, adapting oper
 ations and making room to test\, monitor and refine\; and preference goes 
 to problems involving multi-modal input\, especially Indic languages\, and
  to use cases built on LLMs. Applications are open to nonprofits across se
 ctors\, with priority to climate-focused organisations whose AI use cases 
 could improve programme delivery\, decision-making or community outcomes. 
 Apply via the Google Form linked from the announcement before 10 August 20
 26.\n\nOrganisation: Project Tech4Dev\nLocation: Remote cohort programme. 
 No geographic restriction is stated in the call\, but the framing is India
 -centred: preference goes to problems involving multi-modal input and Indi
 c languages.\nAward: FLAG\, UNVERIFIED: no grant amount\, stipend\, fee or
  compute credit is stated. What the call describes is hands-on implementat
 ion support\, mentorship and expert guidance across three sprints\; partic
 ipating NGOs must invest one person's full bandwidth for the duration.\nDu
 ration: Six months\, tentatively mid-September 2026 to January or February
  2027 (subject to change)\n\nMore: https://projecttech4dev.org/ai-cohort-p
 rogram-3-0-empowering-ngos-to-scale-impact-with-ai/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://projecttech4dev.org/ai-cohort-program-3-0-empowering-ngos-to-sc
 ale-impact-with-ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
LOCATION:Remote cohort programme. No geographic restriction is stated in th
 e call\, but the framing is India-centred: preference goes to problems inv
 olving multi-modal input and Indic languages.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:collider-program-manager-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260823
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260824
SUMMARY:Deadline: Collider: Program Manager (AI safety fieldbuilding\, New 
 York)
DESCRIPTION:Collider\, the New York coworking and convening space for AI sa
 fety and other high-impact work\, is hiring a Program Manager to design\, 
 run and scale the programmes that grow the AI safety talent pipeline and i
 nfrastructure in New York. The role owns programmes end to end\, from conc
 ept to launch to iteration\, working with researchers\, practitioners\, ed
 ucators\, funders and partner organisations. WHAT YOU DO: design and opera
 te fieldbuilding programmes (fellowships\, training programmes\, workshops
 \, conferences\, cohort courses and new formats)\; own budget\, timeline\,
  vendor relationships\, participant experience and post-programme evaluati
 on\; recruit and coordinate mentors\, instructors\, advisors and participa
 nts\, and build the pipelines and selection processes that hold quality as
  things scale\; define and track outcomes with metrics beyond participant 
 satisfaction\, and iterate on the evidence\; maintain relationships across
  research labs\, university groups\, training programmes (MATS\, ARENA\, B
 lueDot\, SPAR\, AI Safety Camp are named)\, grantmakers and adjacent commu
 nities\; represent Collider externally\; write programme reports\, strateg
 y memos\, post-mortems and public materials\; and work with leadership on 
 which programmes to launch\, scale or sunset. REQUIRED: 3+ years of progra
 mme or project management\, ideally running cohort\, fellowship or trainin
 g programmes\; substantive understanding of the AI safety field\, its orga
 nisations\, its main research agendas (alignment\, interpretability\, eval
 uations\, governance) and its open problems\; strong operational instincts
 \; an entrepreneurial\, initiative-taking approach to judgement calls abou
 t people\, partnerships and priorities\; and clear written communication. 
 NICE TO HAVE: prior experience at an AI lab\, AI safety organisation\, uni
 versity group\, training programme or grantmaker\; a background in educati
 on\, talent development or community building\; enough ML or alignment lit
 eracy to follow a paper's argument\; policy literacy on the AI governance 
 landscape\; or a background in finance\, law or another professional field
  Collider wants to build pipelines into. PROCESS: apply via the form in th
 e job document by 23 August 2026 (applications are reviewed on a rolling b
 asis and an offer may be made before the deadline\, so applying early is a
 dvised)\, then a 15 to 25 minute screening call with hiring partner Impact
  Ops\, an interview with the prospective manager\, a paid one-day in-perso
 n work trial at the Collider coworking space\, and 2 to 3 references. Repo
 rts to the Director.\n\nOrganisation: Collider\nLocation: Hybrid in New Yo
 rk City\, USA. No visa sponsorship: candidates must already have the right
  to work in the US.\nAward: USD 150\,000 to 180\,000 base salary\, plus he
 alth insurance\, 401(k) and 20 days paid time off. The one-day in-person w
 ork trial is paid.\nDuration: Full-time\, permanent\; start date flexible\
 n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1cP3r4slpewE6_5wsJQYTPAg_qKsRX
 4WYDSy7bfYxMd0/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://docs.google.com/document/d/1cP3r4slpewE6_5wsJQYTPAg_qKsRX4WYDSy
 7bfYxMd0/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hybrid in New York City\, USA. No visa sponsorship: candidates mus
 t already have the right to work in the US.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:privacy-camp-2026-call-for-sessions@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260809T215233Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Privacy Camp 2026 (EDRi): Call for Sessions\, The Origins
  of Future Technologies
DESCRIPTION:Privacy Camp holds its 14th edition on 13 October 2026 in Bruss
 els and online\, organised by European Digital Rights (EDRi) with the Rese
 arch Group on Law\, Science\, Technology & Society (LSTS) at Vrije Univers
 iteit Brussel\, Privacy Salon vzw\, the Institute for European Studies at 
 Université Saint-Louis Bruxelles\, and the Racism and Technology Center. 
 THEME: 'The Origins of Future Technologies: Towards communities of digital
  self-determination'. The call starts from the premise that digital future
 s do not appear from nowhere but are built through land\, labour\, capital
 \, infrastructure\, law\, public money\, political choices and social stru
 ggle\, and asks where technologies come from\, who gets to shape them\, an
 d what conditions make digital self-determination possible. It situates th
 is against the EU's 2026 Tech Sovereignty Package (semiconductors\, artifi
 cial intelligence\, cloud infrastructure and data centres) and the wider d
 eregulatory agenda including the Digital Omnibus\, the AI Omnibus and refo
 rms affecting data protection\, privacy\, labour and environmental safegua
 rds\, asking whose version of sovereignty is actually being discussed. TOP
 IC AREAS: (1) Material conditions in the production of technologies\, cove
 ring the natural resources needed across the supply chain (critical compon
 ents such as semiconductors\, water for data centres) and how GDPR\, ePriv
 acy and AI Act safeguards shape the material conditions of AI from data ex
 traction and biometric processing through to data centres\, labour and pub
 lic infrastructure\; labour conditions\, including who mines critical mate
 rials\, union-busting across the tech supply chain\, and coalitions betwee
 n tech workers\, unions\, migrant justice groups\, climate movements and d
 igital rights organisations\; and capital\, meaning the venture capital fo
 rces driving the current AI hype and consolidating through defence\, secur
 ity and militarisation. (2) Political vision in the use of technologies\, 
 covering military use and how militarisation and securitisation shape the 
 EU deregulation debate\, and the political ideologies driving technology d
 evelopment\, including what digital self-determination means for communiti
 es facing racialised discrimination\, surveillance\, occupation\, border v
 iolence\, workplace control or environmental harm. (3) Moving technologies
  forward through communities\, covering community innovation for digital s
 elf-determination (distributed and self-hosted data storage\, AI built by 
 and for community needs\, community-led\, public or cooperative digital in
 frastructure)\; law\, deregulation and rights (resisting the Digital Omnib
 us and specifically the Data Omnibus\, defending GDPR and ePrivacy safegua
 rds\, protecting access\, transparency and automated decision-making right
 s\, enforcing existing digital laws\, supporting collective litigation\, a
 nd challenging the framing of rights as obstacles to EU competitiveness)\;
  and building power by connecting grassroots movements across tech worker 
 organising\, labour\, migrant\, LGBTQIA+\, climate\, economic justice and 
 landback movements. FORMATS: interactive workshops are particularly encour
 aged\, meaning small-group work\, peer exchange and collaboration across s
 ectors\, coalitions and disciplines\, moving from talk to strategic action
 \; panel discussions are also welcome\, especially those centring diverse 
 voices and grounded lived experience with room for meaningful audience eng
 agement. SUBMISSION GUIDELINES: state a clear objective for the session an
 d what a good outcome would be\; make it as interactive as possible\; supp
 ort a diversity of voices and multiple perspectives\; average session leng
 th is 50 minutes\; propose the format that actually suits the idea rather 
 than forcing a panel into a workshop or vice versa. For workshops\, make t
 he objectives\, structure and facilitation clear and have participants wor
 k together in small groups towards a specific goal\; the organisers provid
 e additional guidance\, support and feedback for workshops. For panels\, l
 ist a maximum of 4 speakers covering academia\, civil society and decision
 -maker perspectives\, and indicate which speakers have confirmed at least 
 in principle. TIMELINE: proposals due via the online form by 10 August 202
 6 at 09:00 CEST\; the Content Committee notifies applicants before 21 Augu
 st 2026\; confirmation to proceed required by 25 August\; draft programme 
 published 4 September\; speakers and final session description confirmed b
 y 10 September at the latest (earlier confirmation improves the odds that 
 the EUR 400 reimbursement budget covers speaker travel and accommodation)\
 ; final programme published 28 September. The 2026 Content Committee is An
 dreea Belu (EDRi)\, Gloria González Fuster (LSTS\, VUB)\, Jill Toh (Racis
 m and Technology Center)\, Sanne Stevens (Justice\, Equity and Technology 
 Project) and Alexandra Giannopoulou (DFF). Programme enquiries: andreea.be
 lu@edri.org. Venue and reimbursement enquiries: guillermo.peris@edri.org.\
 n\nOrganisation: European Digital Rights (EDRi)\, with LSTS at Vrije Unive
 rsiteit Brussel\, Privacy Salon vzw\, the Institute for European Studies a
 t Université Saint-Louis Bruxelles\, and the Racism and Technology Center
 \nLocation: Brussels\, Belgium\, and online (hybrid). Event on 13 October 
 2026.\nAward: NO speaker fee or honorarium. Where needed\, up to EUR 400 p
 er speaker or session organiser towards transport to and accommodation in 
 Brussels\, reimbursed against invoices and travel tickets\; those with org
 anisational support are encouraged to cover their own expenses. No budget 
 beyond this cap. Free to propose and free to attend (registration open).\n
 Duration: One-day conference on 13 October 2026. Average session length 50
  minutes.\n\nMore: https://privacycamp.eu/privacycamp26-call-for-sessions/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://privacycamp.eu/privacycamp26-call-for-sessions/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brussels\, Belgium\, and online (hybrid). Event on 13 October 2026
 .
END:VEVENT
